{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QQjbga98eMhKNL6esScBQj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Branching on a closed set of states in Kotlin — the short version\n2\t\n3\t**The rule.** When you branch on a value whose set of cases is closed — an `enum class`, or a\n4\t`sealed` hierarchy — write the decision as **one `when` in expression position with no `else`**.\n5\tExpression position means the `when`'s value is *used*: returned, assigned to something, passed\n6\tas an argument, or the last expression of a lambda. Anything but discarded.\n7\t\n8\t**Why that exact shape and not merely \"a `when`\".** Kotlin requires a `when` **used as an\n9\texpression** to be exhaustive. So the compiler — not a reviewer, not a test — holds the case\n10\tlist. Add a constant to the enum tomorrow and this site stops compiling until someone decides\n11\twhat the new case does.\n12\t\n13\tThe three shapes that lose that property, all of which compile today and all of which route a\n14\tnew constant down a fallback path without telling anybody:\n15\t\n16\t1. an `if` / `else if` / `else` chain,\n17\t2. a `when` that carries an `else` branch,\n18\t3. a `when` in **statement** position — its value discarded, used by nothing.\n19\t\n20\t**What to do with the leftover cases you do not care about.** Do not reach for `else`. Name them\n21\tand give them the shared outcome explicitly: `IN_A, IN_B, IN_C -> refuse(...)`. The list is\n22\tlonger to read and it is the whole point — the next person who adds a constant is forced to\n23\tdecide, because the compiler asks them.\n24\t\n25\t**One decision, one construct.** Splitting the same decision across two `when`s, or an `if`\n26\twrapping a `when`, gives back the guarantee in a different way: neither construct alone is the\n27\tcase list any more, so neither is compiler-enforced against the enum.\n28\t\n29\tDetails, with the compiler's own behaviour and the three shapes side by side:\n30\t`documents/kotlin-exhaustive-when.md`.\n31\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0550d73b-9293-4205-b6ed-b9aed21a8b97","uuid":"c3007784-c6e5-4f13-a4e6-cd684f5d6565","timestamp":"2026-09-26T18:36:37.427Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/jr/lwzz65cx5pndqqdgzhnym1pc0000gn/T/observatory-run-dcdce8bb-390f-40fa-8647-b4ec39901bdb/.ai/knowledge/summaries/kotlin-exhaustive-when.md","content":"# Branching on a closed set of states in Kotlin — the short version\n\n**The rule.** When you branch on a value whose set of cases is closed — an `enum class`, or a\n`sealed` hierarchy — write the decision as **one `when` in expression position with no `else`**.\nExpression position means the `when`'s value is *used*: returned, assigned to something, passed\nas an argument, or the last expression of a lambda. Anything but discarded.\n\n**Why that exact shape and not merely \"a `when`\".** Kotlin requires a `when` **used as an\nexpression** to be exhaustive. So the compiler — not a reviewer, not a test — holds the case\nlist. Add a constant to the enum tomorrow and this site stops compiling until someone decides\nwhat the new case does.\n\nThe three shapes that lose that property, all of which compile today and all of which route a\nnew constant down a fallback path without telling anybody:\n\n1. an `if` / `else if` / `else` chain,\n2. a `when` that carries an `else` branch,\n3. a `when` in **statement** position — its value discarded, used by nothing.\n\n**What to do with the leftover cases you do not care about.** Do not reach for `else`. Name them\nand give them the shared outcome explicitly: `IN_A, IN_B, IN_C -> refuse(...)`. The list is\nlonger to read and it is the whole point — the next person who adds a constant is forced to\ndecide, because the compiler asks them.\n\n**One decision, one construct.** Splitting the same decision across two `when`s, or an `if`\nwrapping a `when`, gives back the guarantee in a different way: neither construct alone is the\ncase list any more, so neither is compiler-enforced against the enum.\n\nDetails, with the compiler's own behaviour and the three shapes side by side:\n`documents/kotlin-exhaustive-when.md`.\n","numLines":31,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31}}}
